libFuzzer support for DynaJS
=============================

BUILD WITH A SANITIZER. THIS IS NOT OPTIONAL.

  CONFIG_CLANG=y CONFIG_NATIVE_MODULES=y make <target>

DO NOT ADD CONFIG_ASAN=y HERE -- IT IS STRICTLY WEAKER FOR FUZZ TARGETS.
An `ifndef CONFIG_ASAN` guard blanks FUZZ_DEFAULT_SAN, so that flag buys
`address` and LOSES `undefined`. Measured: fuzz_stdlib built with it has 14234
asan symbols and ZERO __ubsan_handle_*, against 9 for every default-built
target. `make fuzz-all` asserts both sanitizers are present, by symbol rather
than by flag.

A LOCAL BUILD NOW GETS ONE BY DEFAULT -- `make <target>` links
-fsanitize=address,undefined. Set FUZZ_NO_DEFAULT_SAN=y only if you supply your
own (OSS-Fuzz does, via CFLAGS), and the build will warn that it did.

Without a sanitizer, libFuzzer catches CRASHES and nothing else -- an
out-of-bounds read produces no report at all, so the entire bug class these
targets exist to find is invisible. This is not a matter of efficacy.

MEASURED, 2026-08-01: a deliberately planted one-byte heap overread survived
**5229 executions** with no report, because `nm fuzz_stdlib | grep -c asan` was
0. FOUR targets (dyns, lz4, net, scram) already hardcoded a sanitizer; the
SEVEN that went through LIB_FUZZING_ENGINE did not, and they were the ones that
missed it. All eleven are now consistent.

Rebuilt with a sanitizer the plant fires at `dyna-scrape.c:80` -- and the same
rebuild found a REAL pre-existing heap-use-after-free in dyn_ulid_time (the
error path freed the string, then read it to format the message). An earlier
version of this file called the sanitizer an efficacy improvement, which is why
nobody set it.

THE SEED MUST REACH THE CODE. The plant above did not fire on
`Disallow: /a%` alone -- with no `User-agent:` line no group is active, so the
rule is never pushed and the decoder never runs. `corpus_robots/` seeds shapes
that do reach it.

So: a clean run from a build without a sanitizer says almost nothing. Re-run
the corpus under a plain `make` build (address+undefined) before trusting any
past result -- NOT under CONFIG_ASAN=y, which drops the undefined half.

Targets
-------

  fuzz_bytecode
  fuzz_compile
  fuzz_dyns
  fuzz_eval
  fuzz_json
  fuzz_lz4
  fuzz_module_export
  fuzz_net
  fuzz_regexp
  fuzz_scram
  fuzz_stdlib

CONFIG_NATIVE_MODULES=y is required for fuzz_stdlib and fuzz_net: the parsers
they cover live in NAT_MODULE_OBJS and a default build compiles none of them.

Running
-------

  ./fuzz_stdlib                       # cold
  ./fuzz_stdlib corpus_dir/           # seeded -- ALWAYS prefer this
  ./fuzz_eval -dict=src/fuzz/fuzz.dict    # with the dictionary
  ./fuzz_stdlib -max_total_time=60 -rss_limit_mb=4096

SEED FROM A CORPUS. A fuzzer starting cold spends its budget rediscovering the
grammar before it can reach the interesting code, and for anything behind a
length-and-terminator format that can exceed the whole run -- so a real bug
reads as "the target does not catch it", which is the wrong conclusion about
the wrong thing.

A target is unproven until you inject a fault and watch it fire
---------------------------------------------------------------

Add the bug, run, confirm the report names it, revert. A target that has never
caught anything may be reaching nothing at all -- which is exactly what the
5229-run result above turned out to mean.

Reproducers (crash-*, leak-*, timeout-*, oom-*) are written to the CWD and are
gitignored. Read `git status` before committing after a fuzz run: a
fault-injection session produces reproducers for bugs that do not exist.
